&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hebrews 9:11-14 (NIV): &quot;But when Christ came as high priest of the good things that are now already here, he went through the greater and more perfect tabernacle that is not made with human hands, that is to say, is not a part of this creation. He did not enter by means of the blood of goats and calves; but he entered the Most Holy Place once for all by his own blood, thus obtaining eternal redemption. The blood of goats and bulls and the ashes of a heifer sprinkled on those who are ceremonially unclean sanctify them so that they are outwardly clean. How much more, then, will the blood of Christ, who through the eternal Spirit offered himself unblemished to God, cleanse our consciences from acts that lead to death, so that we may serve the living God!&quot;
&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;Jesus&#39; blood, offered through the eternal Spirit, cleanses our consciences, not just our flesh, from &quot;dead works&quot; to serve the Living God. Crucially, Jesus&#39; death was also the means by which the New Covenant was brought into full force. Just as a will (or testament) becomes legally binding only after the death of the testator (the one who made it), so Christ&#39;s death empowered the New Covenant:
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Hebrews 9:16-17 (NIV): &quot;In the case of a will, it is necessary to prove the death of the one who made it, because a will is in force only when somebody has died; it is never in force while the one who made it is still living.&quot;
&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;Jesus, by dying and resurrecting, became the ultimate Testator, making His New Covenant (the Gospel) eternally enforceable. He now lives as our Advocate, ensuring the terms of this testament are applied to us. This means we, as New Covenant believers, are not under the curse of the Law (which primarily applied to those under the Law), but rather under the grace accessed by faith. We, who were not called God&#39;s people under the Law, have now become His people through faith in Christ.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Our blessing comes directly through Abraham&#39;s faith, by way of Christ, extending to all who believe, including Gentiles:
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Galatians 3:8-9 (NIV): &quot;The Scripture foresaw that God would justify the Gentiles by faith, and announced the gospel in advance to Abraham: &#39;All nations will be blessed through you.&#39; So those who rely on faith are blessed along with Abraham, the man of faith.&quot;
&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Galatians 3:14 (NIV): &quot;He redeemed us in order that the blessing given to Abraham might come to the Gentiles through Christ Jesus, so that by faith we might receive the promise of the Spirit.&quot;
&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;The promises of God in Christ are not &quot;yes&quot; and &quot;no&quot; or &quot;wait,&quot; but &quot;yes&quot; and &quot;amen&quot; (2 Corinthians 1:20). Our spiritual promises, received by faith, are eternally secured. So, how do we strengthen this faith and make it potent in our lives? Abraham&#39;s example, again, provides the blueprint:
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Romans 4:20-21 (NIV): &quot;Yet he did not waver through unbelief regarding the promise of God, but was strengthened in his faith and gave glory to God, being fully persuaded that God had power to do what he had promised.&quot;
&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;We strengthen our faith by constantly giving glory to God for His promises, even when they haven&#39;t yet manifested. This act of worship fuels our faith. We must also cultivate an unshakeable conviction that God is not only willing but also perfectly able to perform everything He has promised. Nothing is too big or too difficult for Him. When you walk in this understanding, giving glory and being fully convinced, you activate the righteousness credited to you through Christ. This isn&#39;t merely a theological concept; it becomes your nature and lifestyle, manifesting as acts of giving, forgiveness, and powerful prayer.
&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: left;&quot;&gt;The Gospel of Salvation brings you to the person of Jesus; the Gospel of the Kingdom teaches you the principles to live powerfully and prosperously within His reign. By faith, we access every realm of God, shake places through prayer, and ultimately grow into the &quot;fullness of the measure of the stature of Christ&quot; (Ephesians 4:13).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;</description><link>https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/2025/06/faith-part-3.html</link><author>noreply@blogger.com (Bright Dumashie)</author><media:thumbnail xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhyvMzCKg492JyQhs_Rmm5E4c0zkOF4IcQ4aqtVwz8k2ZKt8y5s3EY-V0tEfv5zUsWCSWUSIRbC25SyXMeYJ7QZ8oZWjY2kvzLjGZNI-E9sgZx2EQ3gPmDhuO-T3FxGj1L2JhWR6nqtP4C7jtMF54THaKo6sqgc_rIspM-6-ImcXQAMRlyj-IFnHaNPe8UJ/s72-w503-h381-c/Leonardo_Phoenix_10_Generte_an_image_with_the_topic_tilte_FAI_3%20(2).jpg" height="72" width="72"/><thr:total>0</thr:total></item><item><guid isPermaLink="false">tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-5469563646699054757.post-1981368848284370291</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Jun 2025 09:41:00 +0000</pubDate><atom:updated>2025-06-03T09:41:03.550+00:00</atom:updated><category domain="http://www.blogger.com/atom/ns#">Faith &amp; Salvation</category><title>FAITH  [ Part 2 ]</title><description>&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h1 style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;FAITH&amp;nbsp; [ Part 2 ]&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/#/search/label/Faith%20and%20Salvation&quot; imageanchor=&quot;1&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot; style=&quot;margin-left: 1em; margin-right: 1em;&quot;&gt;&lt;img border=&quot;0&quot; data-original-height=&quot;1120&quot; data-original-width=&quot;1120&quot; height=&quot;452&quot; src=&quot;https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjNE_gSXbxxkBH9PKxF462aiI5mx69Duh7-bSyoaoeISXKXOVTWxKhEmANCQoaCZ7FUH-WKzSQ4pP6PcLuw6rUL-NP_mcTEu0QI8Ugfb20IM4ezc0Xo1gghLIcj6ocbbJ4eoeNS8V6SAAQUYlfpIH2LjoCelPM9xH_DNulPx0x5WDZG2EJYb_K493bbawMl/w452-h452/Leonardo_Phoenix_10_Generte_an_image_with_the_topic_tilte_FAI_3%20(1).jpg&quot; width=&quot;452&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Every sturdy structure begins with a strong foundation. In our journey of faith, a deep and accurate understanding of God&#39;s Word is not merely beneficial; it&#39;s indispensable. As believers, we are called to move beyond superficial knowledge and delve into the foundational truths that anchor our spiritual lives.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;The Indispensable Foundation: Looking Unto Jesus&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The very bedrock of our faith is found in where we fix our gaze. The Bible clearly states:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hebrews 12:1-2 (KJV): &quot;Therefore, since we are surrounded by such a great cloud of witnesses, let us throw off everything that hinders and the sin that so easily entangles. And let us run with perseverance the race marked out for us, looking unto Jesus, the author and finisher of our faith, who for the joy set before him endured the cross, scorning its shame, and sat down at the right hand of the throne of God.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This scripture, particularly the phrase &quot;looking unto Jesus, the author and finisher of faith,&quot; is the ultimate foundation. He is not merely our author and finisher, but the originator and perfecter of faith itself. The life of Abraham serves as a powerful case study for this foundational faith – a faith that God Himself authenticated. Jesus said, &quot;If you were Abraham’s children, you would do the works of Abraham&quot; (John 8:39), validating Abraham&#39;s walk as a model for all who are of faith.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;To be effective Bible students, we must always seek out the fundamentals – the foundational truths and accounts of how God first dealt with individuals and their responses to His experiential knowledge. Without this, we risk misunderstanding entire concepts.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Decoding Scriptural Truths: Beyond Surface Reading&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;A common pitfall in biblical understanding is the failure to discern distinct truths. Consider, for example, the two statements about Jesus: &quot;the firstborn from the dead&quot; and &quot;the firstborn of all creation.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Firstborn from the Dead: This refers to Jesus&#39; unique resurrection. He died, was buried, and resurrected with the same body, but glorified. This is distinct from Lazarus, who was raised but still subject to the same earthly body and limitations. Jesus&#39; resurrection introduced a newness of life and body.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Firstborn of All Creation: This speaks to Jesus&#39; pre-existence and His role in creation. Before anything was ever created, He was the first to be brought forth out of creation, not created by it in the same sense as other beings. When the Bible says, &quot;In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth&quot; (Genesis 1:1), Jesus, the Christ, was part of that initial creative act, the pattern and design within God&#39;s mind, before being manifested. He is God&#39;s &#39;firstborn&#39; in the sense of ultimate pre-eminence and origin over all creation.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This nuanced understanding extends to the very act of God&#39;s creation. God first conceives patterns and designs in His mind (creation – bara, calling something out of nothing), and then He speaks them into being, forming or making them manifest (yatsar or asah). Adam and Eve, for instance, were created in God&#39;s mind before being formed from the dust of the earth. Understanding this distinction is fundamental to grasping God&#39;s creative power and process.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;The Temple Unveiled: A Blueprint of God&#39;s Plan&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;One of the richest sources of foundational truth, often misunderstood, is the blueprint of God&#39;s dwelling place: the Temple. The Old Covenant temple, built by Moses and Solomon, was a shadow of the heavenly reality. To understand God&#39;s ultimate plan, we must look to the original.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;1. The Throne Room (The Most Holy Place):&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This is where God, the Lord of Hosts, sits on His throne, the Mercy Seat. He is guarded by two cherubs, whose golden decoration signifies purity and holiness. This is the place of justice and judgment, but also grace.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Hebrews 4:16 (KJV): &quot;Let us therefore come boldly unto the throne of grace, that we may obtain mercy, and find grace to help in time of need.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;We approach this throne not physically, but by faith. Faith is the transportation system of the spiritual realm, allowing our spirits to appear before God and make our petitions.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;2. The Altar:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Positioned before the Most Holy Place, the altar is crucial for purification and atonement.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Leviticus 17:11 (KJV): &quot;For the life of the flesh is in the blood: and I have given it to you upon the altar to make an atonement for your souls: for it is the blood that maketh an atonement for the soul.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Jesus&#39; blood, the purest and holiest, poured upon the altar, made atonement for the whole world. It propitiates for our sins and cleanses our conscience, presenting us holy before God. The altar also features Brimstone fire, symbolizing God&#39;s purging presence, as seen in Isaiah&#39;s encounter:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Isaiah 6:6-7 (KJV): &quot;Then flew one of the seraphims unto me, having a live coal in his hand, which he had taken with the tongs from off the altar: And he laid it upon my mouth, and said, Lo, this hath touched thy lips; and thine iniquity is taken away, and thy sin purged.&quot;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This demonstrates how atonement, through fire and blood, purges sin, making us ready to serve.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;3. The Sanctuary: Components of Covenant Life:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Within the sanctuary, we find other significant elements:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Altar of Incense: This represents the prayers and sacrifices of the people, offered through the priest. Our giving – tithes, offerings, first fruits, seeds – are part of this incense, offered through our High Priest, Jesus.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Malachi 1:11 (KJV): &quot;For from the rising of the sun even unto the going down of the same my name shall be great among the Gentiles; and in every place incense shall be offered unto my name, and a pure offering: for my name shall be great among the heathen, saith the Lord of hosts.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This foreshadowed Jesus, Jehovah our Salvation, to whom all worship and offerings are ultimately directed.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Table of Showbread: With its twelve loaves, this represents the twelve tribes of Israel, symbolizing how God&#39;s provision and presence are available to His people. In the New Covenant, this expands to all spiritual Jews – those born of Christ, both outwardly and inwardly (Romans 2:28-29).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Seven Golden Candlesticks: In Revelation, John the Revelator received a vision where he saw Jesus amidst seven golden lampstands:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Revelation 1:12-13 (KJV): &quot;And I turned to see the voice that spoke with me. And having turned I saw seven golden lampstands; and in the midst of the seven lampstands One like the Son of Man...&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Revelation 1:20 (KJV): &quot;The mystery of the seven stars which you saw in My right hand, and the seven golden lampstands: The seven stars are the angels of the seven churches, and the seven lampstands which you saw are the seven churches.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;These represent the Church in its fullness across all dispensations – the body of Christ, carrying the light of God in the world.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Understanding these components of the temple helps us grasp God&#39;s design for our redemption, access to Him, and our role as the Church. We, through Jesus, have boldness to walk to the very throne of God, having a &quot;higher high priest&quot; who has passed through the heavens (Hebrews 4:14).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Living a Life of Unwavering Faith: Abraham&#39;s Example Revisited&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Armed with these foundational truths, how then do we live a life of unwavering faith? Abraham&#39;s example, as detailed in Romans 4, provides profound insights:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;Romans 4:13-24 (NIV): &quot;It was not through the law that Abraham and his offspring received the promise that he would be heir of the world, but through the righteousness that comes by faith. For if those who depend on the law are heirs, faith means nothing and the promise is worthless, because the law brings wrath. And where there is no law there is no transgression. Therefore, the promise comes by faith, so that it may be by grace and may be guaranteed to all Abraham’s offspring—not only to those who are of the law but also to those who have the faith of Abraham. He is the father of us all. As it is written: &#39;I have made you a father of many nations.&#39; He is our father in the sight of God, in whom he believed—the God who gives life to the dead and calls into being things that were not. Against all hope, Abraham in hope believed and so became the father of many nations, just as it had been said to him, &#39;So numerous will your offspring be.&#39; Without weakening in faith, he faced the fact that his body was as good as dead—since he was about a hundred years old—and that Sarah’s womb was also dead. Yet he did not waver through unbelief regarding the promise of God, but was strengthened in his faith and gave glory to God, being fully persuaded that God had power to do what he had promised. This is why ‘it was credited to him as righteousness.’ The words &#39;it was credited to him&#39; were written not only for him but also for us, to whom God will credit righteousness—for us who believe in him who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead. He was delivered over to death for our sins and was raised to life for our justification.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;b&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Abraham’s faith wasn&#39;t based on human ability or the law, but on God&#39;s promise and grace. He didn&#39;t waver in unbelief; instead, he was strengthened in faith by two powerful actions:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Giving glory to God: Abraham continually gave thanks and praise to God for the promise, even before its manifestation. This act of worship in expectation fortified his spirit.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Being fully convinced: He was absolutely persuaded that God, who had made the promise, was also perfectly able to perform it. He understood God&#39;s immense power and faithfulness.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This righteousness, credited to Abraham, is also imputed to us who believe in Jesus Christ. Our justification comes through faith in Him. Therefore, even when circumstances seem contrary, we declare what is true in the spirit, not what appears to be true in the flesh. We do not stagger or doubt, but stand firm, declaring God&#39;s promises over our lives and situations.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Cultivating a Life of Deep Faith&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;To grow in grace and knowledge, as Peter exhorted (2 Peter 3:18), we must worship God with understanding, grounded in His Word. The more we delve into the rich, foundational truths of scripture, the more we exhibit the true nature of a son of God. We are called to be kings and priests, reigning in life through Christ.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;So, let us emulate Abraham. Let us give glory to God for every promise, and be fully convinced that the One who has promised is more than able to perform it. Our faith in Jesus is not merely for material gain, but these blessings do come to accomplish the greater purpose of our faith in Him. Believe, declare, and watch God move!&lt;/p&gt;</description><link>https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/2025/06/faith-part-2.html</link><author>noreply@blogger.com (Bright Dumashie)</author><media:thumbnail xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEjNE_gSXbxxkBH9PKxF462aiI5mx69Duh7-bSyoaoeISXKXOVTWxKhEmANCQoaCZ7FUH-WKzSQ4pP6PcLuw6rUL-NP_mcTEu0QI8Ugfb20IM4ezc0Xo1gghLIcj6ocbbJ4eoeNS8V6SAAQUYlfpIH2LjoCelPM9xH_DNulPx0x5WDZG2EJYb_K493bbawMl/s72-w452-h452-c/Leonardo_Phoenix_10_Generte_an_image_with_the_topic_tilte_FAI_3%20(1).jpg" height="72" width="72"/><thr:total>0</thr:total></item><item><guid isPermaLink="false">tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-5469563646699054757.post-5854092657172531664</guid><pubDate>Tue, 03 Jun 2025 09:15:00 +0000</pubDate><atom:updated>2025-06-03T09:26:13.168+00:00</atom:updated><category domain="http://www.blogger.com/atom/ns#">Faith &amp; Salvation</category><title>FAITH  [ Part 1 ]</title><description>&lt;h1 style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;FAITH&amp;nbsp; [ Part 1 ]&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/#/search/label/Faith%20and%20Salvation&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot; style=&quot;margin-left: 1em; margin-right: 1em;&quot;&gt;&lt;img border=&quot;0&quot; data-original-height=&quot;1120&quot; data-original-width=&quot;1120&quot; height=&quot;399&quot; src=&quot;https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhWvsDykKvuVtfa_mCrSwNkvw2axOhFsgcNbBHWP1_xP1Lv6tb7rjaCqwgPJmmfrApS8srhWgXkfPgv2VSKFaN38JP3dKf0nSOXLHhzom-K_LQCyh999BNVOG95CNaTGAbwIUCMf1mDYZPGjrFpcFl_Bf3bFNM26gGaiGT6rYE8XeqAnyvsgQ3RRk8NF_Nc/w475-h399/Leonardo_Phoenix_10_Generte_an_image_with_the_topic_tilte_FAI_3.jpg&quot; width=&quot;475&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;This moment marks a significant shift in our journey. For years, I have shared wisdom nuggets on faith, offering glimpses into its profound truths. Now, the time has come to systematically unfold faith as a comprehensive topic, drawing directly from the volumes of scripture and the deep revelations packaged within my spirit. Prepare your spirit; we are about to ascend to new heights.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The genesis of faith, as first revealed in the Bible, is found in the story of Abraham. His experience lays the foundational understanding of what true faith entails. As we explore the Gospels, Epistles, and Revelations, we will continually refer back to this initial encounter, recognizing it as the blueprint God imparted to Abraham.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;It is crucial, before we proceed, to understand the very nature of God. The Abraham we speak of encountered the Elohim, the Spirit who is the Father of all. At that time, Jesus, the Word of God, had already proceeded from God. We recognize the Lord God Almighty as the Lord Jesus, yet when we speak of the Almighty God, we are referring to Elohim—the one Jesus called “My Father,” the one He referred to as “God is Spirit.” This is the very being upon whose throne Jesus now sits. When we delve deeper into the Word, we understand that a ‘person’ in the biblical sense is a living being who functions independently, possessing their own mind, will, intellect, desires, aspirations, ambitions, and emotions. This definition is essential, for it clarifies that God is not comprised of three distinct persons each seated on their own throne; that is not the ideology of God.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Similarly, we must define biblical terms with precision. When the Bible speaks of “life” (nephesh), it refers to the soul. Thus, we encounter living souls and those who are dead, awaiting their ultimate extinction. Just as a tree might have its surface killed, yet hope for survival remains if its roots are in the ground, so too when a man dies from the Earth, he is not totally extinct from the universe or other worlds. The soul of a man, whether they have worshipped Jesus or not, descends into the abyss. Though in the futuristic sense, their hope for resurrection on the last day leads only to a second death, the Bible still refers to them as “the dead.”&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Scripture explicitly states that “hell will give up the dead that are in them; the seas will give up the dead that are in them; the land will give up the Dead; the grave will give up the dead that are in them.” It is vital to note that the grave is an opening into the spiritual realm on Earth, but hell is distinct from the grave. God’s Word provides clear distinctions between hell, the seas, the grave, and the Earth. All these places will surrender their dead for the Lake of Fire. Having been taken to witness these places, I can confirm that the abyss is void of creation; it is a place of the dead, where nothing is manufactured. Condemned souls go to hell, a place where Jesus Himself went. Though condemned, His inherent power, derived from His divine DNA, allowed Him to react even there. This was an angelic power. However, it is when the Holy Ghost came upon Him that He accessed creative power. Jesus Himself is life; He declared, “I have life in myself to lay down my life and I have life to pick my life again.” Yet, it was God who raised Jesus from the dead, for Jesus offered His life upon the altar to be resurrected by the Father.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This leads us to a fundamental truth: faith is acted by action. It is not passive; it demands engagement.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Now, let us turn our attention to the book of Hebrews, where a profound instruction is given regarding our walk of faith.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Therefore we also, since we are surrounded by so great a cloud of witnesses, let us lay aside every weight, and the sin which doth so easily beset us, and let us run with patience the race that is set before us,&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith; who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame, and is set down at the right hand of the throne of God.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;— Hebrews 12:1-2 (KJV)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The “cloud of witnesses” speaks of a multitude of onlookers. These witnesses are not only men but also trees, animals, water systems, stars, and angels—all living beings that testify to God’s reality and Jesus’s sonship. Some of these witnesses you may not see, and even those you see, you may not discern their activity. For instance, a tree breathes, and the Bible regards certain trees as divine, like the fig tree. These witnesses are preserved from extinction, regardless of global upheavals, because they serve as signs of God’s constant presence and power. Angels, too, are witnesses, protecting and preserving us, even when we are unaware. Everything created by God—from the elements to the living beings—is a cloud of witnesses, testifying to His greatness. This is why God, being Spirit, can manifest as any element He has made.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Since we, the believers, are surrounded by this great cloud of witnesses, the instruction follows: “Let us lay aside every weight and the sin which so easily ensnares us.” What is a “weight”? It is anything that impedes our spiritual progress, preventing us from experiencing the full measure of these witnesses. Anxiety is a weight. The cares of this life—what you will eat, what you will drink, worries about school fees, or financial provisions—these are all weights, demonstrating a lack of faith in God’s ability to provide. A person of faith lives in constant expenditure yet never lacks, for they are supplied by a higher power. It takes someone who can disregard earthly concerns and fix their gaze upon the Spirit.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Sin is distinct from weights, though both ensnare us. Jesus Himself described weights, like those who give seeking human applause, for they have already received their reward. Such a mindset is a weight that draws you away from God. As for sin, it ensnares you, making you unable to advance or enjoy the cloud of witnesses. For example, many endowed with power from on high still struggle with acts like masturbation. While an isolated act may not be a demon, when it becomes a norm, a demon is certainly behind it, pushing you even as a Christian, because it is an inspiration contrary to God’s Spirit. Such a person, though confessing faith, needs deliverance, not just a faith teaching.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Faith, by its very nature, does not acknowledge any act of unrighteousness. You cannot engage in sin and simultaneously claim to possess faith; that is not faith. True faith is inseparable from the righteousness of God. You cannot mix God’s righteousness—His divine nature and lifestyle—with the transgressions of sin. God’s righteousness is absolute; He can use even darkness to fulfill His righteous judgment. He is the ultimate rule-maker; His rules are not subject to our understanding or agreement.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Understanding prophecy is equally vital. There are miracles I could perform, but I must first teach you that such realms exist and that they are possible. Many pastors have seen miracles, but what God has given me to do, you may not have witnessed. I’ve seen pockets instantly filled with money, bundles appearing from nowhere. These things happen because I was taught, and I rehearsed what I was taught. If you dispute it, I stop. If you want it, you must be ready for obedience, for obedience accompanies faith. Faith asks no questions; it simply acts. When a word of faith is issued, the obedient receive it.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The Bible differentiates between a word of knowledge and prophecy. A word of knowledge reveals what happened in the past or what is happening in the present, including one’s thoughts. For example, describing what you wore yesterday or what you are thinking now is a word of knowledge. Prophecy, however, tells you what will happen tomorrow. It comes from God, not from man’s will. It is the foresight of the future, telling us what God has planned (His perfect will, which cannot be changed) or what the enemy has planned to interrupt one’s destiny.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Consider a scenario: God’s spiritual plan for you is a 90-year life. However, demons might have convened when you were young, programming an early termination at 35 years, burying this evil plan in the spirit realm. As you approach that age, a prophet, operating in the spirit of prophecy (which is the testimony of Jesus), might reveal this captured destiny to you. This is not God’s original plan, but what the enemy has plotted. God, in His fatherly responsibility, uses prophecy to alert you. It takes years for the office of the prophet to mature, but the spirit of prophecy can also temporarily baptize individuals to speak to the church.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The office of the prophet can conduct a “pre-financial investigation” into your life, discerning foundational problems, such as familial poverty. If, upon receiving such a revelation and the accompanying instruction, you question it, the angels assigned to implement God’s solution will depart, never to return. God will allow a demon to manufacture circumstances around you, even if you are in church, because you doubted. This applies to all areas, including family curses or destinies of early death. Many believe that “faith” alone will negate these, but true faith, when faced with a revelation of an enemy’s plan, demands specific action and sustained service to realign with God’s ordained destiny.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Our spiritual journey is a race that requires endurance, a race set before us. It is not for the swift or the strong, but for those who run by God’s standards—which is His Word. To endure, we must look unto Jesus, the “Author and Finisher of Faith.” He is the initiator and perfecter of faith itself, not just “our” faith, for faith is a subject, a spirit, a portion of His very being. Your personal faith is ratified and defined by your faith in Jesus and, consequently, your faith in His Word. If you can never dispute the Word of God, then you are truly enduring on the track.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;If you look elsewhere—to psychology, sociology, theology, or even geopolitical conflicts—fear becomes your inspiration, and you are diverted. But for the joy set before Him, Jesus endured the cross, despising its shame, and is now seated at the right hand of God’s Throne. If we desire to sit on thrones and qualify for eternal reward, we must endure afflictions, disturbances, chastisement, castigation, gossip, and lies. If you are not experiencing these, you are not truly doing the work. My own cross involves facing opposition and false accusations, yet I endure, praying for those who persecute me. To know Jesus as He truly is, to become like Him, we must embrace the full mystery of godliness, not the superficial hyper-grace that denies the need for endurance through trials.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;Consider the awesome presence God manifested at Mount Sinai:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;For ye are not come unto the mount that might be touched, and that burned with fire, nor unto blackness, and darkness, and tempest, And the sound of a trumpet, and the voice of words; which voice they that heard intreated that the word should not be spoken to them any more: (For they could not endure that which was commanded, And if so much as a beast touch the mountain, it shall be stoned, or thrust through with a dart:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;— Hebrews 12:18-20 (KJV)&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;This passage describes the Old Covenant experience, where God’s presence was terrifying, marked by fire, blackness, darkness, and tempest. Here, it is important to distinguish between “blackness” and “darkness.” Blackness can be a color or an absence of light that reveals light, like a black curtain. Darkness, however, is a spiritual state, an ideology of misery, pain, anguish, and sorrow. Wars bring darkness; unforgiveness is darkness. Your heart can be filled with darkness, not merely blackness. We are not called to that mountain of terror but to a new covenant, where we can approach God without fear, having laid aside all weights and ensnaring sins, running the race of faith with our eyes fixed on Jesus. Only by embracing these truths can we truly challenge the people of these end times to achieve something greater and better in our day.&lt;/p&gt;</description><link>https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/2025/06/faith-part-1.html</link><author>noreply@blogger.com (Bright Dumashie)</author><media:thumbnail xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhWvsDykKvuVtfa_mCrSwNkvw2axOhFsgcNbBHWP1_xP1Lv6tb7rjaCqwgPJmmfrApS8srhWgXkfPgv2VSKFaN38JP3dKf0nSOXLHhzom-K_LQCyh999BNVOG95CNaTGAbwIUCMf1mDYZPGjrFpcFl_Bf3bFNM26gGaiGT6rYE8XeqAnyvsgQ3RRk8NF_Nc/s72-w475-h399-c/Leonardo_Phoenix_10_Generte_an_image_with_the_topic_tilte_FAI_3.jpg" height="72" width="72"/><thr:total>0</thr:total></item><item><guid isPermaLink="false">tag:blogger.com,1999:blog-5469563646699054757.post-1697775914771827691</guid><pubDate>Mon, 02 Jun 2025 11:55:00 +0000</pubDate><atom:updated>2025-06-02T19:59:07.013+00:00</atom:updated><category domain="http://www.blogger.com/atom/ns#">Discipleship</category><title>The Trinity Doctrine</title><description>&lt;h1 style=&quot;text-align: center;&quot;&gt;THE TRINITY DOCTRINE&lt;/h1&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/#/search/label/Discipleship&quot; rel=&quot;nofollow&quot; style=&quot;margin-left: 1em; margin-right: 1em;&quot;&gt;&lt;img border=&quot;0&quot; data-original-height=&quot;1024&quot; data-original-width=&quot;1536&quot; height=&quot;328&quot; src=&quot;https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhjuI3YVJ8NfPLjW5QVnu7fxYSqakRjoc7w-9DCoRB7KUZ1ZzcwTFld1e_TeIpG-5EXuVP6_KrJu4FvqKsLsqqwIsncnGphEjrvbTZnJqKXvGws7bTIYcK2IkFzTG9KmTJs6A7r0SrNLmC-Oi_0Rjo_2wK67Hf-ZPT3JhVFds2trgQ9BLeGIH1ZVE2nVDPy/w491-h328/ChatGPT%20Image%20Jun%202,%202025,%2011_21_07%20AM.png&quot; width=&quot;491&quot; /&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div class=&quot;separator&quot; style=&quot;clear: both; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;h2 style=&quot;background-color: white; color: #222222; font-family: Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;THE DOCTRINE OF THE TRINITY (Part 1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;script async src=&quot;https://pagead2.googlesyndication.com/pagead/js/adsbygoogle.js?client=ca-pub-8779160448164370&quot;
     crossorigin=&quot;anonymous&quot;&gt;&lt;/script&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;We all intuitively sense that there is a Creator of all things. As believers, we rely solely on the Bible and the revelation given to us through Jesus Christ, who himself declared in&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 14:6 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 14 Jesus said to him, “I am the way, the truth, and the life. No one comes to the Father except through Me.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Every discussion this evening will be 100 percent Scripture-based, interpreted by the Spirit of God and confirmed by other passages of Scripture, ensuring that we grasp true understanding rather than merely reading words on a page.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;From the very beginning, God’s plan has unfolded like a carefully staged drama. Consider Noah in Genesis 6–9: the Flood wasn’t a random event but part of an ordained narrative. Or Abraham’s promise in Genesis 15: “Your descendants will be enslaved for 400 years, but afterward I will bring them out with great possessions.” Every hardship, exile, and victory in Israel’s history was part of God’s script to reveal His character and purposes. When Moses prophesied the coming of the Messiah—“The LORD your God will raise up for you a Prophet like me from your brethren; Him you shall hear” (Deuteronomy 18:15)—he was merely reciting his lines in that grand drama.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Deuteronomy 18:15 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 15. “The Lord your God will raise up for you a Prophet like me from your midst, from your brethren. Him you shall hear,&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Deuteronomy 29:29 reminds us,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Deuteronomy 29:29 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 29 “The secret things belong to the Lord our God, but those things which are revealed belong to us and to our children forever, that we may do all the words of this law.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;There are aspects of God’s ways that remain beyond our full comprehension, yet He has revealed sufficient truth for us to understand His plan.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;That plan extends even to predestination. Romans 8:29–30 says,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Romans 8:29-30 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 29. For whom He foreknew, He also predestined to be conformed to the image of His Son, that He might be the firstborn among many brethren. 30. Moreover whom He predestined, these He also called; whom He called, these He also justified; and whom He justified, these He also glorified.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;From the chosen people of Israel to the Gentiles who would be grafted in, each person has been assigned a role in this divine production. The “mystery of God,” as John writes in Revelation 10:7, will be finished when the seventh angel sounds—prophets have seen only glimpses of it, yet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Revelation 10:7 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 7. but in the days of the sounding of the seventh angel, when he is about to sound, the mystery of God would be finished, as He declared to His servants the prophets.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;“all Scripture is given by inspiration of God” (2 Timothy 3:16), revealing Himself and His grand narrative to us.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp;2 Timothy 3:16 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 16. All Scripture is given by inspiration of God, and is profitable for doctrine, for reproof, for correction, for instruction in righteousness,&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Now, let us address the Trinity itself. Many argue that the word “Trinity” does not appear in the Bible, which is technically correct. Yet Christians use terms like “Trinity,” “Rapture,” or “Resurrection” to define biblical concepts not explicitly named in Scripture. The Council of Nicaea (AD 325) cemented “Trinity” as official doctrine, but did the Bible truly preach three separate gods or three “persons” in one God? Tonight, we will see that the answer is far less complex than many assume.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;First, consider 1 John 5:7–8, often cited to support three co-equal persons:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;1 John 5:7-8 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 7 For there are three that bear witness in heaven: the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit; and these three are one. 8 And there are three that bear witness on earth: the Spirit, the water, and the blood; and these three agree as one.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;On earth, the “Spirit, water, and blood” refer to Jesus’ baptism (Matthew 3:16–17). He was baptized with water, John the Baptist declared Him the Lamb of God (blood), and the Spirit descended like a dove—all bearing consistent testimony to His identity. The voice from heaven, “This is My beloved Son,” was the Father’s singular testimony. In heaven, the triad “Father, Word, Holy Spirit” operates similarly as three forms of testimony to one truth. They do not necessarily imply three separate “persons,” but rather three ways in which the one God reveals Himself.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Next, examine Matthew 28:19:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Matthew 28:19 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 19. Go therefore and make disciples of all the nations, baptizing them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit,&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Notice the wording: “name” (singular), not “names.” This triadic formula can be understood as a threefold identification rather than three co-equal entities. We must interpret this alongside all Scripture to see whether it teaches three distinct divine Persons or a unified identity expressed in three ways.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Let us now turn to Genesis, where the drama begins. Genesis 1:1–2 declares,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Genesis 1:1-2 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 1. In the beginning God created the heavens and the earth. 2. The earth was without form, and void; and darkness was on the face of the deep. And the Spirit of God was hovering over the face of the waters.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;The first word, “God,” is Elohim—a plural form that nevertheless functions as singular. Then we read of “the Spirit of God.” Since “God is Spirit” (John 4:24), why describe “the Spirit of God” as though separate?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 4:24 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 24. God is Spirit, and those who worship Him must worship in spirit and truth.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;This tells us that “Spirit of God” is simply God Himself in action—His own self-expression—rather than a distinct third person.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;In Genesis 1:26–27, we read,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Genesis 1:26-27 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 26. Then God said, “Let Us make man in Our image, according to Our likeness; let them have dominion over the fish of the sea, over the birds of the air, and over the cattle, over all the earth and over every creeping thing that creeps on the earth.” 27. So God created man in His own image; in the image of God He created him; male and female He created them.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Here we see plural pronouns: “Let Us,” “Our image.” Some suggest God spoke to angels, but angels did not participate in creation—they were themselves created later. Therefore, “Us” must refer to another “who was with God” at Creation. In John 1:1–3, we read,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 1:1-3 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 1. In the beginning was the Word, and the Word was with God, and the Word was God. 2. He was in the beginning with God. 3. All things were made through Him, and without Him nothing was made that was made.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;The Word (Logos) was both “with God” and “was God” at the beginning, so when God said “Let Us,” He spoke with the Word. There were two “personalities” at Creation: God (the Spirit) and the Word (who later became the Son).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;John 1:14 tells us,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 1:14 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 14 And the Word became flesh and dwelt among us, and we beheld His glory, the glory as of the only begotten of the Father, full of grace and truth.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;When the Word took on flesh, He assumed the role of the Son. In turn, God became known as the Father in relation to His Son—completing the Father-Son dynamic in the drama of redemption.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Paul clarifies this relationship in Colossians 1:15–19:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Colossians 1:15-19 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 15. He is the image of the invisible God, the firstborn over all creation. 16. For by Him all things were created that are in heaven and that are on earth, visible and invisible, whether thrones or dominions or principalities or powers. All things were created through Him and for Him. 17. And He is before all things, and in Him all things consist. 18. And He is the head of the body, the church, who is the beginning, the firstborn from the dead, that in all things He may have the preeminence. 19. For it pleased the Father that in Him all the fullness should dwell,&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;The “firstborn over all creation” does not mean Jesus was created; it means He is preeminent and superior over all that exists. All creation was through Him, and “the fullness of God” dwelt in Him—fully God, not a separate third person.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Now, where does the Holy Spirit fit? Many believe the Spirit is a distinct third person, but Scripture reveals otherwise. In 2 Corinthians 3:17, Paul states,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;2 Corinthians 3:17 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 17. Now the Lord is the Spirit; and where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;The “Lord” here refers to God the Father—“The Lord is the Spirit.” Thus, when Scripture speaks of the Holy Spirit, it is describing the Father’s active presence, His own “stage name,” when He interacts with creation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Jesus himself made this clear in John 14:10:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 14:10 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 10. Do you not believe that I am in the Father, and the Father in Me? The words that I speak to you I do not speak on My own authority; but the Father who dwells in Me does the works.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;And in Acts 10:38, we read,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp;Acts 10:38 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 38 how God anointed Jesus of Nazareth with the Holy Spirit and with power, who went about doing good and healing all who were oppressed by the devil, for God was with Him.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;If the Father “dwells in” Jesus and does the works, and Acts tells us that God anointed Him “with the Holy Spirit,” then the Holy Spirit is simply the Father’s manifestation on earth. They are one and the same.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Romans 8:11 adds another proof:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Romans 8:11 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 11. But if the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus from the dead dwells in you, He who raised Christ from the dead will also give life to your mortal bodies through His Spirit who dwells in you.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Who raised Jesus from the dead? The Father. Therefore, “the Spirit of Him who raised Jesus” must be the Father. Romans 8:15–16 further confirms,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Romans 8:15-16 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 15. For you did not receive the spirit of bondage again to fear, but you received the Spirit of adoption by whom we cry out, “Abba, Father.” 16. The Spirit Himself bears witness with our spirit that we are children of God.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;When we receive the Holy Spirit, we cry out “Abba, Father,” and that same Spirit confirms our adoption. In essence, the Holy Spirit is the Father testifying to our hearts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Let us return to 1 John 5:7–8 with this understanding: when it speaks of&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt; “three that bear witness in heaven—the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit,”&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt; these are actually two distinct personalizations of one God. The Father and the Word are distinct “persons,” but the Holy Spirit is not a third person—it is the Father in action. Therefore, all three—Father, Word, and Holy Spirit—bear witness to the one God. On earth, “Spirit, water, and blood” likewise bear united testimony to Jesus’ identity at His baptism.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Peter’s confession in Matthew 16:15–17 underscores this unity: Jesus asked, “Who do you say that I am?” Simon Peter answered,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Matthew 16:15-17 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 15. He said to them, “But who do you say that I am?” 16. Simon Peter answered and said, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God.” 17. Jesus answered and said to him, “Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Yet in 1 Corinthians 12:3, Paul writes,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;1 Corinthians 12:3 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 3. Therefore I make known to you that no one speaking by the Spirit of God calls Jesus accursed, and no one can say that Jesus is Lord except by the Holy Spirit.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Peter’s revelation came from “My Father in heaven,” and Paul tells us that only the Holy Spirit can prompt someone to declare “Jesus is Lord.” Once again, the Holy Spirit is the Father—God testifying on earth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Thus far, we have seen that Genesis 1:26’s&lt;b&gt; “Let Us”&lt;/b&gt; refers to God and the Word, not angels. John 1:1–3 identifies the Word as both “with God” and “was God,” co-creative with the Father. The Holy Spirit, as Paul affirms, is the Father Himself. When you assemble these truths, the Bible never truly teaches three co-equal, distinct persons, but rather two distinct personalities—Father and Son—with the Father manifesting as “Holy Spirit” when He interacts with creation. John 10:30 underscores this unity:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 10:30 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 30. I and My Father are one.”&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;It is a Father-Son relationship, not a three-fold personhood.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;We have only just scratched the surface of this profound truth. In our next episode, we will explore further questions: why did Jesus say, “The Father is greater than I” (John 14:28)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 14:28 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 28. You have heard Me say to you, ‘I am going away and coming back to you.’ If you loved Me, you would rejoice because I said, ‘I am going to the Father,’ for My Father is greater than I.&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;What did He mean when He promised, “I will ask the Father, and He will give you another Helper, that He may abide with you forever” (John 14:16)?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;John 14:16 (NKJV)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: center;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; 16. And I will pray the Father, and He will give you another Helper, that He may abide with you forever—&quot;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Who is this “another Helper”—another what? These mysteries find clarity when we view Scripture as the unfolding of God’s divine script.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p style=&quot;background-color: white; text-align: start;&quot;&gt;&lt;span face=&quot;Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif&quot; style=&quot;color: #222222; font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;Thank you for joining this study. Stay blessed, and I will see you next week for Part 2. Good night!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: medium;&quot;&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br /&gt;&lt;/div&gt;</description><link>https://propheticharvesters.blogspot.com/2025/06/the-trinity-doctrine.html</link><author>noreply@blogger.com (Bright Dumashie)</author><media:thumbnail xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" url="https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEhjuI3YVJ8NfPLjW5QVnu7fxYSqakRjoc7w-9DCoRB7KUZ1ZzcwTFld1e_TeIpG-5EXuVP6_KrJu4FvqKsLsqqwIsncnGphEjrvbTZnJqKXvGws7bTIYcK2IkFzTG9KmTJs6A7r0SrNLmC-Oi_0Rjo_2wK67Hf-ZPT3JhVFds2trgQ9BLeGIH1ZVE2nVDPy/s72-w491-h328-c/ChatGPT%20Image%20Jun%202,%202025,%2011_21_07%20AM.png" height="72" width="72"/><thr:total>1</thr:total></item></channel></rss>